Bug lens, hypotheses tested:
1. Cache-before-write in `tagDeviceUI`. `tagDeviceUI` calls `localCache.SetDefault(key)` before `CreateOrUpdateDevice`. When the limit is reached, the first request for a new device gets ErrDeviceLimitReached and the identity is rejected. The cache entry stays, though. Later requests with the same device ID within the 29-minute TTL hit the cache, return nil, and authenticate as anonymous without a DB row. This bypasses the limit, and the same ordering also caches transient DB errors. I reported this.
2. Check-then-insert race in `CreateOrUpdateDevice`. It runs CountDevices and then INSERT with no lock, transaction or constraint. Concurrent new devices can all pass `count < limit` and exceed it. Anonymous traffic is concurrent by nature, and the old async goroutine made this worse. I reported this.
3. Synchronous tagging in `client.go`. The goroutine and its timeout were removed, so every anonymous request that misses the local cache now blocks on a COUNT query plus an upsert, using the request context. This is a latency and DB-load cost in the auth hot path. It is mostly intentional, because the error has to propagate, so I did not report it separately.
4. `TagDevice` now returns errors from `tagDeviceUI`. The only production caller is `client.go`, which handles it, so there is no regression.
5. `updateDevice` returns ErrDeviceLimitReached when 0 rows are affected. That is also true for MySQL when the values are unchanged, because MySQL reports 0 affected rows for a no-op update. Time precision also affects the match. A MySQL no-op update (same ip, user agent and a second-truncated timestamp) can wrongly reject an existing device. This is plausible but speculative, because `updated_at` is always set to a new time. I did not report it.

